@extends('layouts.app')

@section('title')
  Installation
@endsection

@section('content')
  <h1>{{ $title }}</h1>
  <p>Ignite is a Rust crate that adds an admin panel to a Rocket app. The app needs a Rust toolchain, a MariaDB or MySQL database, and, for the panel's assets, Node.js.</p>

  <h2>Requirements</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Rust, stable, with Cargo.</li>
    <li>MariaDB or MySQL. Ignite supports no other database.</li>
    <li>Node.js 22.12 or newer, or 20.19 or newer, for Vite and Tailwind.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Pinned versions</h2>
  <p>Ignite, its docs and its example use the same versions of these crates:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Rocket, from git, at revision <code>3a54d079</code>.</li>
    <li>Propel, <code>propel_rocket</code>, from git, at the full revision <code>e0fb10893095a4b66996d35efb45cf0dd466ccdd</code>. A short revision makes Cargo build two copies. Propel renders the panel's views.</li>
    <li>Diesel, from a fork, at the full commit <code>a07c4b2c20a466ef34f50ca283790f4df20b0699</code>. The fork is Diesel 2.3.14 with one fix, for libmariadb 3.4, which returned an empty first row for some text queries.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Add the crates</h2>
  <p>Add Ignite to <code>Cargo.toml</code>, pinned to a commit you have tested. Use the same Rocket, Propel and Diesel pins as Ignite, so the app builds one copy of each:</p>
  <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-toml"># The Ignite commit this site documents.
[dependencies]
ignite-panel = { git = "https://github.com/lajennylove/Ignite", rev = "4898b969617db02d3c7360b7c56cfa693a05aa03" }
ignite-core = { git = "https://github.com/lajennylove/Ignite", rev = "4898b969617db02d3c7360b7c56cfa693a05aa03" }
rocket = { git = "https://github.com/rwf2/Rocket", rev = "3a54d079" }
propel_rocket = { git = "https://github.com/lajennylove/propel", rev = "e0fb10893095a4b66996d35efb45cf0dd466ccdd" }
diesel = { git = "https://github.com/lajennylove/diesel", rev = "a07c4b2c20a466ef34f50ca283790f4df20b0699", features = ["mysql", "r2d2", "chrono"] }
serde = { version = "1", features = ["derive"] }
chrono = { version = "0.4", features = ["serde"] }
dotenvy = "0.15"

[build-dependencies]
propel_compiler = { git = "https://github.com/lajennylove/propel", rev = "e0fb10893095a4b66996d35efb45cf0dd466ccdd" }</code></pre>
  <p>The derive writes paths that start with <code>ignite_core</code>, so the app needs that crate too. A date field needs <code>chrono</code> with its <code>serde</code> feature. The app's CLI binary uses <code>dotenvy</code> to read <code>.env</code>.</p>
  <p>Apps pin the same Diesel fork and commit, so no <code>[patch]</code> line is needed.</p>

  <h2>Database URL</h2>
  <p>Ignite reads the connection URL from the <code>DATABASE_URL</code> environment variable, in the form <code>mysql://user:pass@host:port/db</code>. It builds the connection pool when the panel is created, and panics at startup if the variable is missing or the database cannot be reached. In development, the variable can be set in a <code>.env</code> file.</p>

  <h2>Migrations</h2>
  <p>The panel owns a <code>users</code> table, which it creates with its own migration. Do not create a <code>users</code> table in your app. Run the panel's migrations at startup, with a connection from the pool:</p>
  <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-rust">use ignite_core::build_pool;

pub fn migrate(url: &amp;str) -&gt; Result&lt;(), Box&lt;dyn std::error::Error + Send + Sync&gt;&gt; {
    let pool = build_pool(url)?;
    let mut conn = pool.get()?;
    ignite_panel::run_migrations(&amp;mut conn)
}</code></pre>
  <p>Call <code>run_migrations</code> once at startup. It is the one way to run the panel's migrations, and running them again does nothing. Do not rely on <code>diesel migration run</code> for them: it cannot see the panel's migrations, which are embedded in the <code>ignite-panel</code> crate.</p>

  <h2>Attach the panel</h2>
  <p>Attach the panel to the Rocket build with the resources the app manages. The panel's routes mount when the app starts, under <code>/admin</code>. The app's own <code>account</code> route is guarded by the panel's <code>AdminUser</code>. See <a href="/docs/panel">Panel</a>:</p>
  <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-rust">mod article;
mod author;
mod reports;
mod views_dir;
mod migrate;
mod order;
mod mount;
mod schema;
mod tag;

use ignite_panel::Ignite;

#[rocket::launch]
fn rocket() -&gt; rocket::Rocket&lt;rocket::Build&gt; {
    rocket::build().attach(
        Ignite::new()
            .resource::&lt;article::Article&gt;()
            .resource::&lt;tag::Tag&gt;()
            .resource::&lt;author::Author&gt;()
            .csrf_exempt(&amp;["/hooks/*"])
            .mount("/admin")
            .fairing(),
    )
    .mount(
        "/",
        rocket::routes![
            reports::account,
            order::checkout,
            order::place_order,
            order::payment_hook
        ],
    )
}</code></pre>

  <h2>Create the first user</h2>
  <p>Each app has a small binary at <code>src/bin/ignite.rs</code>. It loads <code>.env</code> first, as the workspace binary does:</p>
  <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-rust">fn main() {
    // The app reads DATABASE_URL from .env, so the CLI does too.
    let _ = dotenvy::dotenv();
    ignite_panel::cli::main();
}</code></pre>
  <p>Then create a user from the app:</p>
  <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-bash">cargo run --bin ignite -- user create --email you@example.com --name "You"</code></pre>
  <p>The command reads <code>DATABASE_URL</code>, connects, runs the panel's migrations and checks that the email is free. Only then does it ask for the password, twice, without showing it. A configuration error stops the command before the prompt. The password needs at least 8 characters.</p>
  <p>A script can give the password instead. When standard input is not a terminal, or with <code>--password-stdin</code>, the password is the first line of standard input. The password has no environment variable.</p>
  <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-bash">printf '%s' "$PW" | cargo run --bin ignite -- user create --email you@example.com --password-stdin</code></pre>

  <h2>Run it</h2>
  <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-bash">npm install
npm run build   # once: writes public/build, which Rocket serves
npm run dev     # Vite, in one terminal
cargo run       # the Rocket app, in another</code></pre>
  <p>Rocket will not start until <code>public/build</code> exists, so run <code>npm run build</code> once first. In a debug build, views are read from <code>resources/views/</code> at runtime, so an edit shows on the next reload. A release build compiles them, so rebuild after an edit.</p>

  <h2>Deploy</h2>
  <p>A release build renders compiled views, so the panel's views are part of the binary and a deployed app needs no view files. <code>IGNITE_VIEWS_DIR</code> works in debug builds only. An app with views of its own renders them compiled in release, with its own <code>build.rs</code>, as the panel does. See <a href="/docs/views">Views</a>.</p>

  <h2>Secret key</h2>
  <p>Set <code>IGNITE_SECRET_KEY</code> before you start the app. It signs the sign-in and flash cookies. See <a href="/docs/panel">Panel</a> for how to create one, and for what happens without it.</p>
@endsection
